<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Feminine Mystique</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1963">1963</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Feminine Mystique is a book by the American author <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/betty-friedan">Betty Friedan</a>, first published by W. W. Norton in 1963 and widely credited with sparking second-wave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feminism">feminism</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It grew from a survey Friedan made in 1957 of her former Smith College classmates, many of whom were unhappy with their lives as housewives, and from interviews with other suburban housewives and research into psychology, media and advertising. Friedan coined the phrase feminine mystique for the assumption that women would be fulfilled by housework, marriage, sexual lives and children. A bestseller, it was criticised for focusing on white, middle-class women.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-feminine-mystique.jpg" alt="The Feminine Mystique" width="330" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fred Palumbo, World Telegram staff photographer. Restored by Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Betty_Friedan_1960.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
